<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#silk">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Silk is a fine, strong fabric made from the cocoons of silkworms, first produced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It was one of the most valuable goods of long-distance trade across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>, giving its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. It was central to the economies of the Han, Tang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Ming dynasties and to the Parthian, Sasanian, Gokturk, Mongol, Chagatai and Safavid empires, which traded or taxed it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silk.jpg" alt="Silk" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kuebi = Armin Kübelbeck · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silk_raw_01a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
